Implementation Notes
--------------------------------------------------------
 
   This file is used by the SPICE system as follows: programs that make
   use of this I-kernel must `load' the kernel, normally during program
   initialization. Loading the kernel associates data items with their
   names in a data structure called the `kernel pool'. The SPICELIB
   routine FURNSH loads a kernel file into the pool as shown below.

      CALL FURNSH ( I_kernel_name )    --FORTRAN
      furnsh_c    ( I_kernel_name );   --C

   In order for a program to extract data from the pool, the SPICELIB 
   routines GDPOOL and GIPOOL are used. See [4] for more details.

   This file was created and may be updated with a text editor or word
   processor.


IIRS Camera NAIF IDs
--------------------------------------------------------

   The following NAIF IDs are assigned to the camera and individual 
   IIRS detectors:

      Name                NAIF ID
      ---------------------------
      CH2_IIR_I           -152240      
      ---------------------------

Keyword Naming Conventions
----------------------------------------------------------

   All names referencing values in this IK file start with the characters `INS' followed by the NAIF Chandrayaan-2 Orbiter spacecraft ID number(-152) followed by a NAIF three digit ID code for instrument. 
   
   The remainder of the keyword name is an underscore character followed by the unique name of the data item. For example, the boresight direction of CH2_IIR_I is specified by:

         INS-152240_BORESIGHT

   The upper bound on the length of the name of any data item is 32 characters.

   If the same item is included in more than one file, or if the same item appears more than once within a single file, the latest value supersedes any earlier values.


Instrument Overview
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
The Imaging InfraRed Spectrometer (IIRS) is an advanced version of the spectrometers (HySI, M3 and SIR-2) flown onboard Chandrayaan-1. 
IIRS  is  based  on  the  offner  spectrometer  based  optical  configuration  in  which  convex  and blazed  grating  is  used.  Fore  
optics  is  TMA  (Three  Mirror  Anastigmat)  based  optics,  which collects  the  light  focus  on  to  the  slit  with  30um  width.  
Through  slit  light  enters  into  the offner spectrometer. IIRS  detector  is  having  500  x  256  pixels  format  with  30um  square  
pixel.  256  are  used  for spectral  bands  and  500  for  swath.  Optical  system  and  detector  both  caters  to  entire wavelength  
range  0.8  um  to  5.0um.  Order  shorting  filter  (OSF)  is  the  integral  part  of detector assembly.  

               |<------  500 detectors ----->|
               ------------........ ------------   ----
    30 micron  |  |  |  | ........ |  |  |  |  |    ^
               ----------........---------------    |    
			   |  |  |  | ........ |  |  |  |  |    |
               ----------........----------------   |
			   |  |  |  | ........ |  |  |  |  |    |
			   ----------........----------------   |                                .
											   .    | 
											   .    |
               ----------........----------------   256
			   |  |  |  | ........ |  |  |  |  |    |
               ----------........----------------   |
			   |  |  |  | ........ |  |  |  |  |    v
               ----------........---------------   ---  

